# Contributing to Pixel Pantry

## Before You Start

- Read the README and understand the game flow.
- Open the repository's GitHub Issues tab and choose an available issue.
- Check whether the issue is still unassigned before you start.
- Comment on the issue or follow the event organizer's instructions before beginning work to avoid duplicate effort.

## Set Up the Project

Fork the repository, then clone your fork:

```bash
git clone <your-fork-url>
cd pixel-pantry
git checkout -b fix/issue-number-short-description
```

Use GitHub's fork workflow rather than asking for direct write access to the event repository.

Open `index.html` in a browser to run the project locally.

## Make Your Fix

- Reproduce the bug before editing.
- Make the smallest appropriate code change.
- Keep the work in HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript only.
- Avoid unrelated refactoring or adding dependencies.
- Test the affected feature and check that the rest of the game still behaves correctly.

## Submit a Pull Request

After making your fix, commit and push the branch:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Fix #<issue-number>: short description"
git push origin fix/issue-number-short-description
```

Then open a pull request against the event repository's default branch.

The PR description should include:
- The issue being fixed
- A short explanation of the change
- The steps used to test the fix

Where appropriate, use `Closes #<issue-number>` so GitHub can automatically close the issue after the PR is merged.

## Event Rules

- One issue per pull request unless organizers permit otherwise.
- Do not submit unrelated changes.
- Do not copy another participant's solution.
- Keep pull requests small, readable, and focused.
- Follow the issue's acceptance criteria.
- Organizers award points after reviewing and verifying a fix.
- The organizer's decision on scoring and duplicate submissions is final.

## Code of Conduct

Please communicate respectfully, provide constructive feedback, and report problems honestly.

We are here to learn, collaborate, and have fun while building a friendly coding event project.

